WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:09.730
Hey guys.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:14.030
JK Rowling here to make a little clarification
on something that’s of monumental importance

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:15.580
to the world of Harry Potter.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:18.349
Yes, Quidditch players CAN get CTE.

00:00:18.349 --> 00:00:24.250
In fact, there are lots of squibs out there
who weren’t born non-magical, but lost their

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:29.460
abilities after repeated blows to the head
by bludgers.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:33.960
And why hasn’t Hermione raised the alarm
about this, along with house elf liberation?

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.900
Well she’s getting kickbacks from the Quidditch
association who want to make sure those fat

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:42.030
advertising checks from Bertie Botts Every
Flavor Beans keep on coming.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.760
But can’t magic cure CTE, you might ask?

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.940
After all, it fixed Harry’s broken skull.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:51.640
Well it could, but Madame Pomfrey is in bed
with big potion, who want you hooked on their

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:52.800
brain-be-good elixir.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.410
Alright you may have figured out by now that
A) I’m not actually JK Rowling and B)

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:00.969
This video is in regards to her various tweaks
to Harry Potter canon.

00:01:00.969 --> 00:01:05.560
What started as a clarification on Dumbledore’s
personal life mere months after the series

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:10.679
concluded, slowly evolved into an avalanche
of unnecessary canon additions to the Harry

00:01:10.679 --> 00:01:11.950
Potter universe.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:16.650
That avalanche recently gave us the qualification
on the specific intensity of Dumbledore’s

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:22.440
romantic life, but nothing will ever top the
revelation that wizards, historically speaking,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.250
used to poo themselves.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.150
So we had to ask: Why?

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:27.530
Why is this happening?

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:31.780
For fans, craving more details about fictional
worlds we’re invested in is simple, and

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.420
incredibly relatable.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.250
We love these worlds and we want to know more.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:42.319
We start with a question like “Where did
Snoke come from? or “was Cobb still dreaming?”

00:01:42.319 --> 00:01:47.569
Then we move on to big questions like: is
Toad’s mushroom a hat, or part of his head?

00:01:47.569 --> 00:01:52.270
Those questions beget more questions, until
we find ourselves asking the “real” questions,

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:57.930
like: if wizards rejected muggle technology
why are there toilets in Hogwarts?

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:59.290
And that’s why we’re here today.

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:02.950
Of course, JK Rowling isn’t the only huckster
of canon.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:08.000
As Mike Rugnetta notes in his incredible essay
“Canon is an Abyss” these occurrences

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.520
are everywhere.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.640
Nintendo has confirmed that toad’s head
is not a hat and designer from Dungeons and

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:20.150
Dragons has canonically declared that gnome
butts smell like honeysuckle and bromine.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:24.890
More recently, Kevin Feige has canonically
clarified why Nick Fury didn’t call Captain

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:28.010
Marvel during several near-extinction scenarios.

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:32.709
While Rugnetta has argued that Canon is a
bottomless abyss of which there is no end,

00:02:32.709 --> 00:02:37.439
and he’s not wrong, I think there’s another
way to understand our canonical obsession,

00:02:37.439 --> 00:02:41.189
and it’s not JUST about how we interact
with the fictional worlds we love, but how

00:02:41.189 --> 00:02:43.049
we interact with our own world.

00:02:43.049 --> 00:02:45.989
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on Canon.

00:02:45.989 --> 00:03:19.989
And spoilers ahead for Harry Potter.

00:03:19.989 --> 00:03:32.390
And now, back to the show.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:36.579
In terms of canon, we are living in a very
specific historical moment.

00:03:36.579 --> 00:03:38.590
First of all, Shakespeare didn’t have Twitter.

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:41.970
But more importantly, he wasn’t living in
an era of “engagement.”

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:46.900
Brands don’t just make extra canonical content
due to our voracious demand, they often do

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:51.889
it in the name of one of the most important
marketing metrics: “engagement.”

00:03:51.889 --> 00:03:55.889
Engagement is, in the world of brand building,
simply any level of interactivity with a brand

00:03:55.889 --> 00:03:57.469
beyond spectatorship.

00:03:57.469 --> 00:04:01.799
That means likes, shares, forum posts, fan
theories, it’s all engagement.

00:04:01.799 --> 00:04:04.720
Today, the number of eyeballs on your brand
isn’t enough.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:09.779
Social media marketers and Youtubers know
the real way to spread brand recall is to

00:04:09.779 --> 00:04:12.209
have consumers interact with your brand.

00:04:12.209 --> 00:04:16.739
Hence, the birth of alternate reality games
to promote shows, clever subway ads that make

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:21.000
you do puzzles, or the rise of “comments”
and “view time” as meaningful measurements

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.090
of success.

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:26.460
In the world of content production, canon
is engagement, and engagement is money.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:31.630
JK Rowling may not be explicitly out to make
a buck by manufacturing more and more canon,

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:35.810
but the study of canon, the discussion of
canon, and the consuming of canon all create

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:38.590
meaningful engagement for the Harry Potter
brand.

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:43.370
Whether it’s a Gryffindor scarf, a custom
lightsaber, or a collectible pickle rick,

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:45.220
engagement moves inventory.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.580
And this logic has taken over everything.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:51.940
This kind of canon production may have existed
in games like Warhammer or Dungeons and Dragons

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:55.310
back in the day, but it's slowly overtaking
the mainstream.

00:04:55.310 --> 00:05:00.980
Today’s games like Overwatch or Apex Legends,
have whole character biographies and additional

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:03.270
“canon” to consume along with them.

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:08.340
These games are, by their very format plotless,
but plotless games don’t make for good,

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.360
meaningful emotional engagement.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:14.010
And meaningful emotional engagement means
beaucoup bucks.

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:19.020
But I don’t want to pin this on mean companies
emotionally exploiting our need be absorbed

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.090
into a highly-monetized fantasy world.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:24.230
After all, JK Rowling has more than enough
money.

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:28.740
But if we go back to even before stock markets,
we can see how canonical meddling was less

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:33.310
about making money and more about anxiety
over one’s place in the world.

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:36.680
Like Rowling, authors have long been clarifying
their works.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:41.430
Even Cicero, as Classics Professor Caroline
Bishop notes meticulously commented on his

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:46.360
old texts and speeches, clarifying how his
original works should really be read.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:51.240
This meddling inspired others, Virgil, Horace,
and Propertius, to give the reader a helping

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.230
hand in interpreting their own words.

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:57.520
Bishops notes that JK Rowling finds herself
in the company of Nabokov, who wrote his own

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:02.770
endnotes for Ada under a pseudonym, and TS
Eliot, who provided his own notes to his famous

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:04.090
work The Waste Land.

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:08.210
In many cases, clarifying canon spoke to an
authorial anxiety.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:12.970
Cicero, Bishop writes, wanted to cement his
legacy and, because he was a politician, win

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:14.080
more votes.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:18.640
There were few things more important than
legacy to these giants of intellect, and continually

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:23.590
revising their work was their way of trying
to ensure their image lived on.

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:28.110
Unlike Rowling- TS Eliot, Nabokov, Cicero
or Vergil didn’t have twitter, and thus

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:32.910
didn’t have hoards of fans asking just how
intense was Humbert Humbert’s romantic life

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:34.360
was, thank god!

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.750
Now, on one hand, I REALLY don’t want to
know.

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:41.740
But, on the other, the ease of which canon
can be tweaked now with a tweet or a television

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:45.690
appearance means authors are even more prone
to impulsivity.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:49.990
And as we’ll argue, the resulting “abyss”
of canon isn’t just annoying.

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:52.200
It can make our lives deeply nihilistic.

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:55.610
To find out just what the hell we’re talking
about, we’re going to get real metaphysical

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:58.290
with 20th century philosopher Martin Heidegger.

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:01.620
Heidegger’s primary focus was our existence
in the world.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:07.270
He argued that since the Ancient Greeks, philosophers
and literally everyone else deeply misunderstood

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:11.670
the question of “being”, so much so that
he had to invent a bunch of words just to

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:12.740
explain it.

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:17.990
Now, this all gets really jargony and hard
to follow, but most simply, he is less interested

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:23.100
in “being” than what he calls "Dasein",
which translates to “Being there.”

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:28.710
In other words, humans don’t exist as some
isolated entity apart from everything else,

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:30.220
we exist in a world.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:35.140
So to understand our existence, we have to
understand our relationship to the environment

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:36.440
around us.

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:41.880
As such instead of simply “being,” his
writing is full of “being-with” “being-in”

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:43.340
and “being-towards.”

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:48.240
Which brings us to being-in-the-world, the
idea that our existence is fully immersed

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:50.170
in the reality around us.

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:55.161
For Heidegger, we are thrown into the world,
and this thrownness, which is his term, is

00:07:55.161 --> 00:07:57.490
a key component of our being.

00:07:57.490 --> 00:08:01.690
As philosopher Simon Critchley summarizes
“Thrownness is the simple awareness that

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:06.970
we always find ourselves somewhere, namely
delivered over to a world with which we are

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:09.530
fascinated, a world we share with others.”

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:14.380
There is something about the fictional worlds
we get lost in that speaks to this basic question

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:15.440
of being.

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:19.770
When we read Harry Potter, we lose ourselves
in the narrative of a boy destined to be a

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:20.930
great wizard.

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:25.550
And we are, metaphorically speaking, thrown
into this fictional universe, and, along with

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:30.990
other fans, are enthralled and curious about
the laws of magic, the history of Hogwarts,

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:32.010
and so on.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:36.519
For Heidegger, being-in-the-world is about
the world disclosing itself to us.

00:08:36.519 --> 00:08:42.180
In a book like Harry Potter, the author discloses,
much more literally, a world and its logic,

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:45.529
rules, mysteries, emotions, and so on.

00:08:45.529 --> 00:08:49.560
This disclosure happens in dialogue, say when
Hagrid explains to Harry that he’s a wizard,

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:54.800
"You're a wizard harry", when we flashback
to the truth about Snape, or just the simple

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.000
descriptions of sentient paintings and nearly-headless
ghosts.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:02.100
We can say the same of Star Wars, or John
Wick, or any imaginary world.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:06.800
I am your father, as uttered by Darth Vader,
discloses a whole new side to the fictional

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:08.560
universe of Star Wars.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:13.300
So, narratives disclose their worlds to us
not unlike how the world discloses itself

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:15.029
to us, cool.

00:09:15.029 --> 00:09:18.149
But does that mean there’s no difference
between learning that Snape was always on

00:09:18.149 --> 00:09:22.180
Team Harry, and the notion that Wizards used
to poo themselves?

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:26.010
Are they both just satiating a curiosity that
is fundamental to our existence?

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:28.550
Well again, I’m going to argue no.

00:09:28.550 --> 00:09:33.910
In fact, the canon updating via tweets might
even make us all nihilists.

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:37.670
The problem, for Heidegger, is when our understanding
of things becomes “instrumental.”

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:42.010
For instance, if I’m looking to make a table,
a tree starts looking like nothing but a nice

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:43.870
future 2x4.

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:48.240
That understanding can conceal the other facets
of its existence like, its importance in the

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:52.810
ecosystem of a forest, its history starting
from a seed, or even just appreciating it

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:54.080
for its beauty.

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:59.270
Heidegger labels this kind of thinking “technological,”
that the vast array of ways we relate to the

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:04.670
world is reduced to the practical, that a
tree exists solely to make wood, a horse exists

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:09.620
solely to ride, or that fellow humans exist
solely as “networking opportunities.”

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:14.399
In the Potterverse, the problem isn’t necessarily
that we’re intrigued by taxonomies of magical

00:10:14.399 --> 00:10:20.769
creatures, but that we want to reduce a mesmerizing
fantasy into a rational, clearly ordered world.

00:10:20.769 --> 00:10:25.610
What we gain in knowing the history of plumbing
in Hogwarts we lose in our original relationship

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:30.009
to the text, our sense of wonderment, inspiration,
etc.

00:10:30.009 --> 00:10:35.550
For Heidegger, this “technological rationality,”
always conceals as it reveals.

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:39.110
In the real world, this leaves us “Homeless”
as Heidegger says.

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:40.990
But, what does he mean by this?

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:45.110
Well that this narrative we’ve spun of us
looking down upon our ordered world has made

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:50.420
us forget our original relationship of being
immersed in that world, and it starts to feel

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:54.450
alien to us, and that’s the kind of nihilism
Heidegger is talking about.

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:56.360
The world loses meaning.

00:10:56.360 --> 00:11:00.779
As Critchley writes, “Heidegger insists
that we have to thrust aside our interpretative

00:11:00.779 --> 00:11:05.450
tendencies which cover over our everyday experience
of the world and attend much more closely

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:07.930
to that which shows itself.”

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:12.240
So, while we’re busy reading JK’s updates
about the history of Hogwarts plumbing, we

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:17.139
conceal other aspects of the book, like all
the feels of Dobby dying a free elf.

00:11:17.139 --> 00:11:21.509
That, and every revelation usually begets
more questions, and, as a result, there is

00:11:21.509 --> 00:11:24.309
no end to what Rowling or others must reveal.

00:11:24.309 --> 00:11:28.100
The revelation that wizards pooped themselves
until somewhat recently, was begat by a screed

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:32.130
in which JK Rowling felt compelled to explain
why there were bathrooms in Hogwarts.

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:36.319
Wizards, we’ve been told, don’t use muggle
technology, so why do they have plumbing?

00:11:36.319 --> 00:11:40.180
And at that point, instead of letting this
fictional world be, she decided to answer

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:43.040
that plumbing was introduced in the 18th century.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:47.220
So did wizards relieve themselves in magical
bowls or bed pans before then?

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:48.220
No.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:51.649
They just let it out, wherever they were,
before magically disposing of it.

00:11:51.649 --> 00:11:55.490
From here, as I’m sure Heidegger would have
warned if he was caught up on Harry Potter

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:58.170
lore, we might ask: who installed the plumbing?

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:00.560
Why is there mention of bedpans in Prisoner
of Azkaban.

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:04.560
Does this imply that wizards can be too ill
to magically un-soil themselves?

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:07.499
Has Madame Pomphry forgotten the lost art
of depoo-ment?

00:12:07.499 --> 00:12:11.129
Is this the kind of magic that lies hidden
in the restricted section of the library?

00:12:11.129 --> 00:12:14.540
Did muggle-born wizards back in the day, who
were acquainted with an outhouse, ever become

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:17.509
homesick for their ancestral way of doing
their business?

00:12:17.509 --> 00:12:21.750
The point is that, in our obsession with canon,
we can often lose sight of what makes a piece

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:24.680
of fiction amazing in the first place.

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:29.550
Mark Twain recounted, in a very coincidentally
Heideggerian way, about a similar experience

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:31.999
with his childhood love of the Mississippi
River.

00:12:31.999 --> 00:12:37.670
As he became an experienced river boat pilot,
the river lost its mystery and magnificence.

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:41.230
He wrote in “Life on the Mississippi”,
“Now when I had mastered the language of

00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:46.130
this water and had come to know every trifling
feature that bordered the great river as familiarly

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:50.189
as I knew the letters of the alphabet, I had
made a valuable acquisition.

00:12:50.189 --> 00:12:52.290
But I had lost something, too.

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:56.050
I had lost something which could never be
restored to me while I lived.

00:12:56.050 --> 00:13:00.160
All the grace, the beauty, the poetry had
gone out of the majestic river!”

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:03.999
For Heidegger, there lies a solution to this
called “releasement.”

00:13:03.999 --> 00:13:08.699
That is, allowing the world to disclose itself,
in all of its facets, to us.

00:13:08.699 --> 00:13:13.410
For Mark Twain, that is the poetry and grace
of the Mississippi, before he learned to masterfully

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:15.120
navigate its treacherous waters.

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:19.869
And maybe that’s what “letting Harry Potter
be” means - not learning to navigate the

00:13:19.869 --> 00:13:25.259
nooks and crannies of canon to figure out
how Draco eats his steak, but to just appreciate

00:13:25.259 --> 00:13:26.259
it.

00:13:26.259 --> 00:13:28.540
Now importantly, that’s not to say you can’t
engage deeper.

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:34.490
You could read, re-read, and constantly discover
new facets and meanings that the text discloses,

00:13:34.490 --> 00:13:38.250
but that doesn’t mean you should necessarily
reread all 7 books to simply build evidence

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:41.870
for your theory that Harry owes back taxes
for his inheritance.

00:13:41.870 --> 00:13:46.079
And that’s why, for example, my favorite
show of all time is Twin Peaks, but I refuse

00:13:46.079 --> 00:13:48.170
to make a Wisecrack video about it.

00:13:48.170 --> 00:13:52.509
Trying to make logical sense about a show
that is so largely about tone, atmosphere,

00:13:52.509 --> 00:13:58.209
and visual artistry will only ruin those elements
that have captivated me like no other show.

00:13:58.209 --> 00:13:59.529
So, what do you guys think?

00:13:59.529 --> 00:14:05.170
Should we embrace the scatology of Hogwarts
in all of its glory, or just let pooping wizards

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:06.170
lie.

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:17.800
Let us know what you think in the comments,
and as always, thanks for watching, guys. Peace.

